// Antivirus products looked up
//
// Fires when wmic or PowerShell queries the SecurityCenter2 namespace for antivirus,
// antispyware or firewall products. That's a quick way to see what security software is
// running before deciding what to kill.
//
// Runs:      Continuous (NRT)
// Severity:  Medium
// MITRE:     T1518.001
// Tables:    DeviceProcessEvents
// Custom details: AccountName, InitiatingProcessFileName
//
// Needs:
//   - Nothing beyond Defender for Endpoint.
//
// Heads up: the AntiVirusProduct class doesn't exist on Windows Server, so this is a
// workstation rule. Scripts run from a file don't show their content in the command line.
//
// Triage (paste into Recommended actions):
//   - Check what launched it and whether it's a known inventory or RMM tool.
//   - Escalate if the launcher is a script host, Office, a browser or something in a user
//     or temp folder.
//   - Close if it's a known tool, and add it to an exclusion.
//
// Based on: Sigma "Potential Product Class Reconnaissance Via Wmic.EXE", MITRE T1518.001.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ingestion_time() > ago(1d)
| where FileName =~ "wmic.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName startswith "wmic.exe" or FileName =~ "powershell.exe" or FileName =~ "pwsh.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName startswith "PowerShell.EXE" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName startswith "pwsh.dll"
| where ProcessCommandLine matches regex @'(?i)securitycenter2|antivirusproduct|antispywareproduct|firewallproduct'
| project Timestamp, ReportId, DeviceId, DeviceName, AccountDomain, AccountName, AccountSid, FileName, FolderPath, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessParentFileName
